all right  can you say it yes yes  so hello my name is alberto cenino and  i'm going to give you a very brief  overview of fastpay so this is a five  minute talk so i'm not going to explain  you  how it works i'm just going to say what  it is and  hopefully if you get interested you can  talk to me later so fastpay  is the typical example of research work  we do at novi research  so i hope it gives you a good overview  of the kind of work we do  so first of all this is joint work with  matthew and george  both are researchers and engineers at  facebook movie with me  so what is fastpay sofaspace a  distributed system  a bft system it's a payment  infrastructure  and it is extremely low latency there is  this particular point with respect to  all other payment systems that are  distributed  so fastpay works in the general bft  settings  so it's composed of a set of nodes a set  of authorities  three f plus one authorities of standard  byzantine assumptions  out of which at most f can be malicious  and the goal that we want here the  design of us pay  is to make distributed payment system  practical for retail payments at  physical points of sale  this means that we would like to have  distributed payments uh in such a way  that you can  go to a coffee shop buy coffee pay with  this kind of system and have the  payments  finished and settled straight away and  this of course requires extremely low  latency  arguably much lower latency than what  traditional blockchains  and current state-of-the-art blockchains  give you  so what is the current industry standard  in that respect  so first of all we want low latency we  want  bft resilience of course and we want  fast finality and high capacity  fast finality means that we want to have  a system in such a way that once we make  the payments  it's finalized and settled very quickly  and the payment cannot be reverted  and high capacity means that we want a  lot of users to be able to use the  system  at the same time so the current industry  system does not seem to do a good job at  that at the moment  first of all most of them are extremely  centralized  this means that they often work with  very old master slave implementations  and it incurs enormous  operational costs for instance they need  to make sure that the system is always  up and running what happens when this  master goes down  and we need to switch the slave what  happens um  if the right people should get access to  the system but not the malicious people  and so on and so forth  and secondly they have very slow  formality people may think that  your credit card payments are fast but  actually they're not really payments  they're just promises of payments when  you most of the time when you pay with  your credit card  the provider and the banks will just  emit a promise of payments  and banks will settle the net payment  usually at the end of the day  and this again create huge credit risks  because the merchant's bank needs to  make sure that the money will be there  at the end of the day  so fast pay solve those problems but  fast pay is not blockchain  traditionally blockchain runs a  primitive called byzantine consensus  while fast pay is run on a weaker  primitive which is called byzantine  consistent broadcast this means that  fastpay cannot do all the things that a  blockchain does  but what it do is payments and it can do  them with much lower  latency than what byzantine consensus  can give you  and so that's it if you're interested  come talk to me you have here a link to  the full paper  and that has the full system and how it  works  and a link to the code because we  implemented it tested it extensively  and we show the latency and the  throughput that we get  for a different number of nodes and  different testbeds so i'm happy to take  any questions  if there are any i think i'm exactly one  minute left for that

